---

<DocsLayout
  title="Streaming a response"
  subtitle="Subscribe to the event feed, submit a prompt asynchronously, print tokens as they arrive — the pattern every nikcli interface is built on."
  description="Guide to streaming nikcli responses: the event feed, promptAsync, message.part.updated deltas, session.idle, and the same flow with curl."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="shape" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The shape of it</h2>
    <p>
      <code>session.prompt</code> returns the finished message. That is fine for a script and wrong
      for an interface — nobody wants a blank screen for forty seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Streaming is a different arrangement of the same endpoints: <strong>subscribe to the event
      feed first</strong>, then submit the prompt with <code>promptAsync</code>, which returns as
      soon as the work is accepted. Output arrives on the feed.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Call</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td><code>client.event.subscribe()</code></td><td>Before submitting, or you miss the first tokens.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td><code>client.session.promptAsync(…)</code></td><td>Accepts the work and returns immediately.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td><code>message.part.updated</code></td><td>Carries each incremental <code>delta</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td><code>session.idle</code></td><td>The turn is finished.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="subscribe" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Subscribe first</h2>
    <p>
      Streaming methods resolve to an object holding an async iterable: you await the connection,
      then iterate the feed.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { stream } = await client.event.subscribe()

for await (const event of stream) {
  console.log(event.type)
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>event.subscribe()</code> is scoped to the resolved instance.
      <code>client.global.event()</code> is the same feed across every instance the server hosts —
      use it when one process watches several projects.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Order matters">
      <p>
        Subscribing after submitting is a race you will lose on short answers. Open the feed, then
        submit.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="submit" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Submit without waiting</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.session.promptAsync({
  sessionID: session.id,
  parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Explain what this repository does." }],
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      It takes the same input as <code>session.prompt</code> — including <code>agent</code>,
      <code>model</code> and <code>tools</code> — and differs only in returning before the work is
      done.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deltas" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Print the deltas</h2>
    <p>
      <code>message.part.updated</code> fires as a part changes. It carries the whole
      <code>part</code> plus an optional <code>delta</code> — the text added since the last event.
      Print the <code>delta</code>; printing <code>part.text</code> reprints the message from the
      start every time.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type !== "message.part.updated") continue
  if (event.properties.part.type !== "text") continue

  process.stdout.write(event.properties.delta ?? "")
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="delta is optional on purpose">
      <p>
        A part can be replaced wholesale rather than appended to — a tool call completing, say — and
        then there is no delta. Fall back to re-rendering from <code>part</code> when you are
        painting a UI rather than appending to a terminal.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="idle" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Know when it is done</h2>
    <p>
      <code>session.idle</code> carries the <code>sessionID</code> that finished. Filter on your own
      session — on a shared feed, other sessions go idle too.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`if (event.type === "session.idle" && event.properties.sessionID === session.id) {
  break
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Watch <code>session.error</code> as well, or a failed turn looks identical to one that is
      still thinking.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="complete" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The whole thing</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="stream.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
})

const { data: session, error } = await client.session.create({ title: "streamed" })
if (!session) throw error

// 1. open the feed before submitting anything
const { stream } = await client.event.subscribe()

// 2. submit without waiting for the turn to finish
await client.session.promptAsync({
  sessionID: session.id,
  parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Explain what this repository does." }],
})

// 3. print as it arrives, stop when the session goes idle
for await (const event of stream) {
  switch (event.type) {
    case "message.part.updated":
      if (event.properties.part.type === "text") {
        process.stdout.write(event.properties.delta ?? "")
      }
      break

    case "session.error":
      console.error("\\n", event.properties.error)
      process.exit(1)

    case "session.idle":
      if (event.properties.sessionID === session.id) process.exit(0)
  }
}`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="events" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Useful events</h2>
    <p>
      The feed carries everything the server does, not just assistant text. These are the ones an
      interface usually handles.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Carries</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>message.part.updated</code></td><td><code>part</code>, <code>delta?</code></td><td>Streaming output.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>message.updated</code></td><td>The message</td><td>Role, model and token counts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>message.part.removed</code></td><td>ids</td><td>Dropping a part from the view.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session.status</code></td><td>idle / busy / retry</td><td>A spinner that tells the truth.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session.idle</code></td><td><code>sessionID</code></td><td>Turn finished.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session.error</code></td><td>The error</td><td>Turn failed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>permission.asked</code></td><td>The request</td><td>Prompting, or auto-replying.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>question.asked</code></td><td>The questions</td><td>The agent needs an answer to continue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>todo.updated</code></td><td>The todo list</td><td>Progress for multi-step work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>file.edited</code></td><td>The path</td><td>Refreshing an editor view.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session.diff</code></td><td>Diff summary</td><td>A live change count.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The full list is the <code>Event</code> union exported by the SDK, so your editor will
      enumerate it and narrow <code>properties</code> per <code>type</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="curl" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Streaming with curl</h2>
    <p>
      The feed is plain server-sent events, so nothing about this requires the SDK. Note the
      <code>?token=</code> form — an <code>EventSource</code> cannot set headers, so the API accepts
      the token as a query parameter.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`# terminal 1 — watch the feed
curl -N "http://127.0.0.1:4096/event?directory=$PWD"

# terminal 2 — submit a prompt
curl -s -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/$SESSION/prompt_async?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"parts":[{"type":"text","text":"hello"}]}'
    `}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>First tokens missing</td><td>Subscribed after submitting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Text repeats and grows</td><td>Printing <code>part.text</code> instead of <code>delta</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loop never ends</td><td>Not handling <code>session.idle</code>, or not filtering it by <code>sessionID</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hangs mid-turn</td><td>A permission request nobody answered. Handle <code>permission.asked</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Events from other work</td><td>Using <code>global.event()</code>, which spans every instance, instead of <code>event.subscribe()</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nothing at all</td><td>Wrong <code>directory</code>, so you are watching a different instance's feed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Using the API", href: "/docs/guides/api", description: "The quickstart this guide continues from." },
      { title: "API Reference", href: "/docs/api/events", description: "The event endpoints themselves." },
